{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And the people spoke against Elohim and against Moses.</b> Even though they knew that all their guidance was by the word of HaShem and Moses did nothing of his own mind, nevertheless they complained against him because he agreed to the judgment of their Maker and did not plead his supplication before Him to bring them by a proper route to the festival seasons. And it appears that their words here were not aimed at bringing a decree upon themselves in the manner they spoke regarding the spies (Numbers 14:3): ״לָמָה ה׳ וְגוֹ׳ נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה״, but rather like one who complains against his fellow for not doing well — and this is called speaking lashon hara. And therefore HaShem sent upon them serpents, measure for measure, as they said of blessed memory (Ta'anit 8a): they asked of the serpent, etc." "<b>And our soul is wearied, etc.</b> Perhaps they sensed, regarding the detour around the land of Edom, that their spirits were shortened on the journey, and they thought that this was caused by the fact that their provision was the manna and not bread. And the reason is that it is the nature of wayfarers to eat something that is somewhat difficult to digest, for during the course of walking one digests quickly, and once digested one needs nourishment again. And therefore when they eat bread they are strengthened and walk with strength, which is not so with eating the manna, which is a spiritual thing and is digested immediately — and this would cause them hardship on the journey because the stomach is empty. And therefore Scripture prefaced and said <b>to detour around the land of Edom</b>, etc., and this was the cause for the people's feeling that led them to speak and say <b>there is no bread</b>, etc., <b>and our soul is wearied with the insubstantial bread</b> — meaning, that spoils immediately and at once and does not last long enough to be digested. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 19:21) that those who spoke were the remnant of the people upon whom the decree had been issued to die in the wilderness, who had no satisfaction of spirit in the wilderness and were wearied of their lives — unlike those who were to enter, who had satisfaction of spirit in the wilderness."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:41.931537610Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}